02775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=5-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{2}{3}(5+3\\text{i}-(5-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(6\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-102775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fbdccf92f21a7349d5f8d53750e5de002224031c4ed05ea7ba15dd8ed17a4ed"} {"question" "q-102775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-102775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "2\\text{i}"] ["math" "4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-102775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+4\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{2}{5}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=-5-4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{2}{5}((-5+4\\text{i})+(-5-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(-10) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fbdccf92f21a7349d5f8d53750e5de002224031c4ed05ea7ba15dd8ed17a4ed"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-25"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "q-103537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/state.json" "file_sha256" "249efc5131146c2d107fbe719a53774a5f7c65c94cb81109b1ba16b80733721d"} {"question" "e-5904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/example-5904.json" "file_sha256" "37d058f18c0b9489cc1836a4403b5c583fe27233aa019ab47207bd008c98153d"} {"question" "e-5904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/example-5904.json" "file_sha256" "37d058f18c0b9489cc1836a4403b5c583fe27233aa019ab47207bd008c98153d"} {"question" "e-5905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/example-5905.json" "file_sha256" "cf60857ece178b1d41dba6cdaa03e05ba7f12f6d3c18b3af65eeb84f2178b74c"} {"question" "e-5905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =2(-2+3\\text{i}+(-2-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2(-4) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040374/example-5905.json" "file_sha256" "cf60857ece178b1d41dba6cdaa03e05ba7f12f6d3c18b3af65eeb84f2178b74c"}] "usage" {"e-5904" [] "e-5905" []} "basis" 1438 "retractions" [[17592186128911 :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186128911 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 2(-2) &= 0 \\\\\n1 - 2λ + λ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 4\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}\n$$"] [17592186128912 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$."] [17592186128912 :question/worked-solution "Since\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a complex eigenvalue of $A$, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1$.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(λ_{1} + λ_{2}) &= 2(-2 + 3\\textrm{i} + (-2 - 3\\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= 2(-4) \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
